Note: closing the external body triggers the server's out.write IOException in `pump` → controller finally closes downstream → downstream status source close() → raw close → stub probe write throws → disconnect. Good. But timing: the server may not detect the external disconnect until it writes the next event. After the delta, the server is blocked in `stream.next()` (downstream read). It will read the stub's probe ':' bytes (no newline) → parser keeps reading. When the external body closes, the server isn't writing, so it won't notice immediately! The controller's `pump` won't throw until it writes to the closed external stream. So the server keeps waiting on downstream until the downstream read timeout (1000ms) → gets TIMEOUT → service catches → tries to write failed to the external stream → IOException (client gone) → propagates → controller finally closes downstream → stub disconnect. So detection takes ~1000ms + probe. `awaitClientDisconnected(4000)` covers it. Good, but the cancellation is driven by the timeout, not immediate. Still, the test asserts downstream cancellation occurs. Acceptable. Actually, to make it detect the disconnect more promptly, the servlet container may detect the closed connection and the `out.flush()`... but the server isn't writing. So it's timeout-driven. Fine.

